{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_TllhMMKO9f0hjhutJGywogki","content":"410\tThe same fix uses portable guest closures for the private get, equality,\n411\tassoc and empty aliases, refuses unadmitted source callees with the qualified\n412\tnot-callable condition, and rejects invalid continuation captures explicitly.\n413\tA raise resumes only through the compiler's private transfer packet.\n414\tNonempty named arguments still raise yang.call/thrown with the original\n415\tbinding error payload, as required by arbitration (e).\n416\t\n417\tVerification: the four requested JVM namespaces plus the full accumulated\n418\tLaTTe oracle corpus pass together: 147 tests, 2,238 assertions, zero failures\n419\tor errors. This includes both 100/1,000 iteration depth lanes, their non-tail\n420\tnegative control, the new rejection fixtures, and UCF reconstruction under\n421\tthe existing validation and instruction budgets. Kondo reports zero errors\n422\tand the same two existing oracle-test warnings; formatting and whitespace\n423\tchecks pass.\n424\t| jolt ws: a ping after a local close is queued gets no pong (A4 \"every ping produces one pong\" holds until a local close exists) | pump.jolt:597 (handle-event! :ping guard); wave-1 Architect sign-off P3 | jolt (J6) | documented behaviour, closed by J6e's docstring sentences; J6h cites the archived sign-off | P3 |\n425\t| yang.php: compile-time default validation is incomplete: `literal-fits?` handles only string, int, bool and null literals; a float default for an `int` property or parameter (`public int $x = 1.5`, `function f(int $x = 1.5)`), arrays and signed literals pass lowering, and `:unknown` is returned whenever a class, object, callable or iterable member exists, so plainly invalid scalar defaults escape; PHP rejects resolved defaults during compilation | P4 S5 independent review (codex gpt-6.1-sol round 2, P2) and Architect sign-off condition 5; `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` default checks | yang.php | P4 S6 (classify statically evaluable defaults completely; defer only genuinely unresolved expressions) | P2 |\n426\t| yang.php: a later rejected by-reference argument leaks an earlier parameter's ownership: with `function f(D &$a, int &$b)`, `$a = new D`, `$b = 'bad'`, catching `f($a, $b)` and then `unset($a)` destroys the object after the next statement (`cED`) instead of before it (`cDE`), because cleanup surrounds only the body and not parameter initialization | P4 S5 independent review (codex gpt-6.1-sol round 2, P2) and Architect sign-off condition 5; `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` parameter initialization | yang.php | P4 S6 (cover parameter initialization in the unwinding cleanup so every acquired parameter is released on failure) | P2 |\n427\t| yang.php: a later rejected by-reference argument leaks an earlier parameter's acquisition: with `function f(D &$a, int &$b)`, `$a = new D`, `$b = 'bad'`, `catch (TypeError $e)`, then `$e = null; unset($a); echo 'E';`, PHP prints `cDE` (the freed trace releases its copy, `unset` drops the last holder) and yang.php prints `cED` (the acquisition of `$a` made before `$b` failed is never released, because cleanup surrounds only the body, not parameter initialization). Without `$e = null` both print `cED`, PHP because the trace holds the argument (corner C30), yang.php because of this leak | P4 S5 independent review (codex gpt-6.1-sol round 2, P2) and Architect sign-off condition 5, restated by the 17.3 ruling of 2026-10-28; `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` parameter initialization | yang.php | P4 S6 (cover parameter initialization in the unwinding cleanup so every acquired parameter is released on failure); fixture `:decl-byref-later-rejection-releases-earlier-param` (`#{:p4-s6}`) | P2 |\n428\t| yang.php C30: exception traces hold no arguments, so an argument rejected at acquisition is released at the throw where PHP keeps it alive until the exception is freed (departure `cDE` vs `cED`) | 17.3 ruling 2026-10-28 on oracle pass 9; design section 17.2 C30 | yang.php | none scheduled; a trace-frames slice | P3 |\n429\t| yang.php: compile-time default validation was incomplete (a float default for an `int` property or parameter, arrays and signed literals passed lowering, and `:unknown` escaped whenever a class, object, callable or iterable member existed); closed in P4 S6: `init-kind` classifies int, float, string, bool, null, array and signed numeric defaults from the syntax tree, `kind-fits?` holds them to the declared type (an int widens to float; callable members accept strings and arrays), and only a default that names a constant is checked when the object or parameter is installed | P4 S5 independent review (codex gpt-6.1-sol round 2, P2) and Architect sign-off condition 5; `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` default checks | yang.php | closed in P4 S6; `compile-errors-are-named-test` entries and fixture `:decl-signed-literal-defaults-accepted` | P2 |\n430\t| yang.php: a later rejected by-reference argument leaked an earlier parameter's acquisition (`function f(D &$a, int &$b)`, `$a = new D`, `$b = 'bad'`: PHP prints `cDE` once the trace is freed, yang.php printed `cED`); closed in P4 S6: declared parameter types are checked in the body's prologue (`php/check-param-cell!`), inside the cleanup that releases every parameter cell, and a function with a typed parameter always takes the unwinding cleanup path. Without freeing the exception both print `cED` (corner C30) | P4 S5 independent review (codex gpt-6.1-sol round 2, P2) and Architect sign-off condition 5, restated by the 17.3 ruling of 2026-10-28; `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` parameter initialization | yang.php | closed in P4 S6; fixture `:decl-byref-later-rejection-releases-earlier-param` (enabled) | P2 |\n431\t| yang.php: first-class callable syntax (`strlen(...)`, `$o->m(...)`, `A::m(...)`) is a syntax error in the vendored `PhpParser.g4` (an argument list has no bare `...`); `Closure::fromCallable` and the callable forms (`[$o, 'm']`, `'A::m'`, invokable objects) carry the semantics | P4 S6; `antlr/php/PhpParser.g4` `arguments` | yang.php | a grammar patch slice before P4 S10 (php-src selection uses the syntax) | P3 |\n432\t| yang.php: the `callable` type check (`php/callable?`) sees only public methods, because a type check has no calling scope; a private `[$this, 'm']` callable passed to a `callable` parameter from inside its class is rejected, while calling it directly works; closure rebinding does not warn for the unbind-`$this` and internal-class cases, and `static` is accepted only as a return type (as a parameter or property type it never matches) | P4 S6 | yang.php | P4 S10 (departure fixtures from the object permutation alphabet) | P3 |\n433\t| yang.php: destructuring by reference into a property (`[&$o->p] = $a`, row 47) is not lowered: the destructuring pattern accepts variable and element targets only; fixture `:obj-row47-destructure-by-ref-into-typed-property` is authored and gated by `:p4-row47` | checkpoint B audit (fixture package); `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` `pattern-of` | yang.php | P4 S7 or a destructuring slice before checkpoint C (the gate lifts when the pattern accepts property targets) | P2 |\n434\t| yang.php: `exit`/`die` is refused (`:constant`), so row 51 (`exit` inside a destructor proceeds to shutdown, corner C9) cannot run; fixture `:obj-row51-exit-in-destructor` is authored and gated by `:p4-exit` | checkpoint B audit (fixture package); design row 51 | yang.php | P4 S7 (the exit escape and the pending-exception destruct halt) | P2 |\n435\t| yang.php: a direct write to a typed static property (`A::$s = 'x'`) is not type-checked; only a reference to it is (a source keyed by class and property, row 46), so assignment through the reference is checked and the direct assignment is not | checkpoint B audit (fixture package) | yang.php | P4 S10 (departure fixture from the object permutation alphabet) or earlier if the sprop write path gains a coe